%I A401959 #5 Nov 15 2026 21:11:03
%S A401959 3,13,137,761,2719,150083
%N A401959 Primes p such that the iteration of T(z) = (z + 2)*(z^3 + 2*z - 1) + z collapses every element of GF(p) to -2.
%C A401959 T(z) - z = (z + 2)*(z^3 + 2*z - 1) and T(z) + 2 = z*(z + 2)*(z^2 + 2). The cubic z^3 + 2*z - 1 has discriminant -59; its splitting field is the Hilbert class field of Q(sqrt(-59)) (class number 3).
%C A401959 For p = 13 = -P(-2), with P(z) = z^3 + 2*z - 1, the point -2 is a double fixed point. For p > 13, -2 is the only fixed point of T in GF(p) iff P is irreducible mod p, i.e., iff p is represented by the form 3*x^2 + x*y + 5*y^2; in particular every term p > 13 satisfies Kronecker(-59, p) = 1. Representations: 3 = (1,0), 137 = (-3,5), 761 = (12,7), 2719 = (29,4), 150083 = (153,112).
%C A401959 No other terms below 10^8 (exhaustive full functional-graph traversal of GF(p) for every prime p); a bounded-step orbit scan up to 2*10^9 found no further terms.
%H A401959 Igor M. Zhuk, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23420627">Unital Collapse Primes in Arithmetic Dynamics: Total Backward-Orbit Collapse for T(x) = (x^2 - 2)^2 mod p, Galois Stratification, and Fermat-Totient Asymptotics</a>, Zenodo preprint (2026).
%H A401959 Igor M. Zhuk, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23856837">Total-Collapse Primes of Polynomial Maps over GF(p): Eighteen Families and Two Related Sequences</a>, Zenodo preprint (2026); <a href="https://github.com/Nondual-Observer/total-collapse-primes-oeis">GitHub verification suite</a>.
%F A401959 p is in the sequence iff #{z in {0..p-1} : T^(p)(z) == p - 2 (mod p)} = p, where T(z) = (z + 2)*(z^3 + 2*z - 1) + z mod p and T^(k) denotes the k-th iterate of T.
%e A401959 For p = 3: -2 == 1 (mod 3), and T(0) = 1, T(1) = 1, T(2) = 1 (mod 3), so all 3 elements of GF(3) reach -2 in 1 step; thus 3 is in the sequence.
%e A401959 For p = 13: -2 == 11 (mod 13), T(0) = 11, and every element of GF(13) reaches 11 within 6 steps under iteration of T(z) mod 13; thus 13 is in the sequence.
%e A401959 For p = 137: p = 3*(-3)^2 + (-3)*5 + 5*5^2 = 137, P_59(z) = z^3 + 2*z - 1 is irreducible mod 137, and all 137 elements of GF(137) collapse to -2 == 135 (mod 137); thus 137 is in the sequence.
%o A401959 (Julia)
%o A401959 isprime(n::Int) = n > 1 && all(d -> n % d != 0, 2:isqrt(n))
%o A401959 function isok(p::Int)
%o A401959     (!isprime(p) || p == 2 || (p > 13 && powermod(-59, p >> 1, p) != 1)) && return false
%o A401959     f(c) = mod(mod(c*(c + 2), p) * mod(c*c + 2, p) - 2, p)
%o A401959     st = zeros(UInt8, p); st[1] = st[mod(p - 2, p) + 1] = 2
%o A401959     for z in 1:p-1
%o A401959         st[z + 1] == 2 && continue
%o A401959         c = z; while st[c + 1] == 0; st[c + 1] = 1; c = f(c); end
%o A401959         st[c + 1] == 1 && return false
%o A401959         c = z; while st[c + 1] == 1; st[c + 1] = 2; c = f(c); end
%o A401959     end
%o A401959     return true
%o A401959 end
%o A401959 println([p for p in 2:200000 if isok(p)])
%Y A401959 Cf. A401173.
%K A401959 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A401959 1,1
%A A401959 _Igor Zhuk_, Nov 10 2026
